<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. M. Coetzee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1940–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as John Maxwell Coetzee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">J. M. Coetzee is a South African-born novelist whose spare, morally searching fiction includes Waiting for the Barbarians, Life &amp; Times of Michael K and Disgrace. He is the first writer to win the Booker Prize twice, in 1983 and 1999, and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 2003. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 2002 and became an Australian citizen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-m-coetzee.jpg" alt="J. M. Coetzee" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yu Chu Chin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Maxwell_Coetzee_at_Elder_Hall,_Adelaide_(DSCF0416).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
